    id: "yaws",
    name: "Yaws and other endemic treponematoses",
    tldr: "A childhood bacterial infection of skin and bone that one dose of azithromycin cures, making it a formal eradication target.",
    summary:
      "Yaws is the most common of the endemic treponematoses — a group that also includes bejel (endemic syphilis) and pinta — and affects children (about 75–80% of cases are in under-15s, peaking at ages 6 to 10) in warm, humid, poor rural communities, causing painless skin lesions that progress, if untreated, to destructive lesions of skin, bone and cartilage years later. A single oral dose of azithromycin cures it as effectively as an injected penicillin, which is what makes mass treatment and eradication feasible. WHO's 2012 Morges strategy aimed to eradicate yaws; progress has been interrupted by resurgence, particularly in the Pacific, and two obstacles persist: serology cannot distinguish yaws from syphilis (and about 40% of yaws-like ulcers are actually Haemophilus ducreyi), and azithromycin-resistant strains have emerged in Papua New Guinea, threatening the single-drug strategy.",
